Mauvely OS: let a PAM module ask a second question after the password

Plasma Login Manager answers every PAM prompt with the password the greeter sent
(Display::slotRequestChanged: one prompt gets the password, two get the user name
and the password). This lets a module ask something else after the password
-- a code, a PIN -- and shows it, with the module's own text, in the greeter.

Against: upstream plasma-login-manager v6.8.1 (2026-10-18), for when Fedora's
plasma-login-manager moves from 6.7.x to 6.8. It is the same change as
plm-second-prompt.patch (6.7.5, Fedora f44) with the three hunks that 6.8
already has or moved dropped or re-made: PAM messages are shown in Main.qml,
frontend/greeter/backend/Messages.h is gone (one common/Messages.h), and
GreeterState.qml's properties moved. Applies to v6.8.1 with `git apply`.

What changes, in the order a question travels:
  helper  PamBackend: inside pam_authenticate(), the first secret prompt is the
          password whatever its text; a later prompt that does not look like the
          password again is a LOGIN_CHALLENGE (new AuthPrompt::Type).
  daemon  Display::slotRequestChanged sends a LOGIN_CHALLENGE to the greeter
          (DaemonMessages::Prompt: QString message, quint32 hidden) and waits;
          GreeterMessages::Respond (QString) answers it (Display::respond).
  greeter GreeterProxy: prompt() signal, respond() slot. GreeterState/Login.qml:
          a second field in the place of the password box, labelled with the
          module's text. Main.qml: the input is given back when a prompt arrives.

An empty answer refuses the question. A module should use PAM_TEXT_INFO for
anything that is not the verdict: PAM_ERROR_MSG still tells the greeter the login
failed. `plasma-login-greeter --test` with PLASMALOGIN_MOCK_CODE=1 shows the
second field without PAM: password "mypassword", code "123456".
